n at only 3%. A modest shift toward Democrats from the national environment keeps the margin near the low teens rather than deeper.", "watch": "A sharp further deterioration in Trump's approval in red North Carolina districts could tighten the race, though Harris remains a heavy favorite.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Mark Harris (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Mark Harris, the Republican incumbent, is heavily favored to hold North Carolina's 8th District against Democrat Colby Watson. The district carries a Republican partisan lean of about ten points, and Harris has raised over $1 million with more than $500,000 on hand, while Watson has raised under $40,000 with minimal cash. Every major expert rater calls the race Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance of an upset.\n\nThe main uncertainty is the national environment: opinion pieces have noted softening approval for the president in some of North Carolina's reddest districts, which could narrow the margin somewhat. Even so, with no public polling showing a competitive race and such a lopsided resource gap, Harris remains a clear favorite to win another term.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0383, "margin": -11.62}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0153, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0236, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0345, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0223, "margin": -13.0}]}